<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Güyük Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1206">1206</a>–<a href="/years/1248">1248</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Güyük Khan was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, reigning from 1246 to 1248. A son of Ögedei, he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1233, when his army captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puxian-wannu">Puxian Wannu</a> of Eastern Xia, and later took part in the western campaign against the Rus'. He was elected after a long regency by his mother Töregene and died while marching west, apparently to confront his cousin Batu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan.jpg" alt="Güyük Khan" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdullâh Sultân (atelier). Shîrâz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guyuk_khan_from_Persian_miniature.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Güyük was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and he served as an officer under both. He married Oghul Qaimish of the Merkit clan. In 1233, with his maternal cousin Alchidai and the general Tangghud, he conquered the short-lived Dongxia Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puxian-wannu">Puxian Wannu</a>, a rebellious Jin official. According to Ata-Malik Juvayni he was a Christian, though Juvayni does not name the denomination.</p><p>He took part in the invasion of Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> from 1236 to 1241 with his cousin Batu and his half-brother Kadan, leading his corps at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and the siege of the Alanian capital Maghas. At the victory banquet he quarrelled violently with Batu, and Ögedei recalled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, threatened him with execution and reprimanded him for fighting within the family and mistreating his soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Ögedei died in 1241 his widow Töregene acted as regent and worked to secure the throne for Güyük. Batu withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to influence the succession, but Güyük was elected in 1246 and enthroned on 24 August near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. Between 3,000 and 4,000 visitors came from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and eastern Europe, among them the papal envoy Giovanni da Pian del Carpine, Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav II of Vladimir</a> and the future Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-iv">Kilij Arslan IV</a>. In reply to the envoy he sent a letter demanding that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> and the kings of Europe come and pay homage.</p><p>Güyük reversed several unpopular edicts of his mother and ordered an empire-wide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. He had Töregene's favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> executed, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s brother Temüge put to death, and executed several high officials of the previous regime. He confirmed Andrey II as grand prince of Vladimir and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-vii-ulu">David Ulu</a> senior king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. In 1247 Cilician Armenia became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state, and in Korea Amuqan's force pillaged the peninsula until 1250.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1248 Güyük launched an expedition against the ulus of Jochi, Batu's territory and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, but he died on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, before reaching the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Some sources say the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochids</a> poisoned him, and some modern historians think he died of natural causes, as his health had declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> reports a violent brawl with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiban">Shiban</a>. His widow Oghul Qaimish became regent, but the succession passed in 1251 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>, nominated by Batu. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1271 he had Güyük placed on the official record as Dingzong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Güyük Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1233 a Mongol army under <b>Güyük</b> captured Puxian Wannu, and the state was absorbed into the Mongol Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-xia">Eastern Xia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khamag Mongol. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
